{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 11 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 11 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nThis is the easy version of the problem. The only difference between the easy version and the hard version is the constraints on $$$n$$$. You can only make hacks if both versions are solved.A permutation of $$$1, 2, \\ldots, n$$$ is a sequence of $$$n$$$ integers, where each integer from $$$1$$$ to $$$n$$$ appears exactly once. For example, $$$[2,3,1,4]$$$ is a permutation of $$$1, 2, 3, 4$$$, but $$$[1,4,2,2]$$$ isn't because $$$2$$$ appears twice in it.Recall that the number of inversions in a permutation $$$a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n$$$ is the number of pairs of indices $$$(i, j)$$$ such that $$$i &lt; j$$$ and $$$a_i &gt; a_j$$$.Let $$$p$$$ and $$$q$$$ be two permutations of $$$1, 2, \\ldots, n$$$. Find the number of permutation pairs $$$(p,q)$$$ that satisfy the following conditions:  $$$p$$$ is lexicographically smaller than $$$q$$$.  the number of inversions in $$$p$$$ is greater than the number of inversions in $$$q$$$. Print the number of such pairs modulo $$$mod$$$. Note that $$$mod$$$ may not be a prime.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 11 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n\r\n\r\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\r\nimport java.util.Arrays;\r\nimport java.util.Scanner;\r\n\r\nimport static java.lang.Integer.min;\r\n\r\npublic class e1542easy {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        Scanner s = new Scanner(System.in);\r\n\r\n        solve(s.nextInt(), s.nextInt());\r\n    }\r\n\r\n\r\n    public static void solve(int n, int mod) {\r\n        ArrayList<int[]> inversions = new ArrayList<>();\r\n        inversions.add(new int[]{1});\r\n\r\n        int last[];\r\n        int next[];\r\n        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\r\n            last = inversions.get(i - 1);\r\n            next = new int[i * (i + 1) / 2 + 1];\r\n\r\n            int e;\r\n            for (int j = 0; j < next.length; j++) {\r\n                e = 0;\r\n                for (int k = min(j, i); k >= 0; k--) {\r\n                    if (j - k < last.length) {\r\n                        e += last[j - k];\r\n                        e = e % mod;\r\n                    }\r\n                }\r\n                next[j] = e;\r\n            }\r\n\r\n            inversions.add(next);\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\r\n            //System.out.println(Arrays.toString(inversions.get(i)));\r\n        }\r\n\r\n\r\n        int perms[] = new int[n];\r\n        perms[0] = 0;\r\n\r\n        int c;\r\n        int inv[];\r\n        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\r\n            c = (perms[i - 1] * i) % mod;\r\n\r\n            inv = inversions.get(i - 1);\r\n            for (int j = 0; j < inv.length; j++) {\r\n                for (int k = j + 2; k < inv.length; k++) {\r\n                    c = (c + inv[j] * inv[k] * (k - j - 1) * (2 * i - (k - j - 2)) / 2) % mod;\r\n                }\r\n            }\r\n\r\n            perms[i] = c;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        System.out.println(perms[n - 1]);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    public static int fact(int n, int mod) {\r\n        int erg = 1;\r\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\r\n            erg = (erg * i) % mod;\r\n        }\r\n        return erg;\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Arrays;\nimport java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class e1542easy {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner s = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int n = s.nextInt();\n        int mod = s.nextInt();\n        solve(n, mod);\n    }\n\n    public static void solve(int n, int mod) {\n        if (n == 1) {\n            System.out.println(0);\n            return;\n        }\n\n        // dp[i][inv] = number of permutations of length i with exactly inv inversions\n        long[][] dp = new long[n + 1][];\n        dp[0] = new long[]{1};\n        dp[1] = new long[]{1};\n\n        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n            int maxInversions = i * (i - 1) / 2;\n            dp[i] = new long[maxInversions + 1];\n            \n            for (int inv = 0; inv <= maxInversions; inv++) {\n                long count = 0;\n                // Place the largest element (i) at position j (0-indexed from right)\n                // This adds (i-1-j) inversions\n                for (int prevInv = 0; prevInv <= inv && prevInv < dp[i-1].length; prevInv++) {\n                    int addedInv = inv - prevInv;\n                    if (addedInv >= 0 && addedInv < i) {\n                        count += dp[i-1][prevInv];\n                    }\n                }\n                dp[i][inv] = count % mod;\n            }\n        }\n\n        long result = 0;\n        // Count pairs (p, q) where p < q lexicographically and inv(p) > inv(q)\n        for (int invP = 0; invP <= n*(n-1)/2; invP++) {\n            for (int invQ = 0; invQ < invP; invQ++) {\n                long countP = (invP < dp[n].length) ? dp[n][invP] : 0;\n                long countQ = (invQ < dp[n].length) ? dp[n][invQ] : 0;\n                result = (result + countP * countQ) % mod;\n            }\n        }\n\n        System.out.println(result);\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.3\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-1.1920922133867862e-06, -4.458328112377785e-05, -2.9802276912960224e-06, -7.70062324590981e-05, -0.012366757728159428, -9.894321920000948e-06, -0.00020454221521504223, -0.1068340465426445, -0.0031976778991520405, -1.4305104514278355e-06, -5.960446742392378e-06, -3.576278118089249e-07, -0.024807140231132507, -0.000554288737475872, -2.50339189733495e-06, -1.823885577323381e-05, -1.2159273865108844e-05, -0.0009660820942372084, -0.00010918975021922961, -0.0002811751910485327, -5.125986263010418e-06, -0.0015274535398930311, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -3.814689989667386e-06, -2.3245540432981215e-05, -5.364403477869928e-06, -8.702239938429557e-06, -8.594620157964528e-05, -6.556489552167477e-06, -0.0001722425949992612, -1.9073468138230965e-06, -1.311301275563892e-06, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -4.768370445162873e-07, -8.940656698541716e-06, -4.0531076592742465e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.311301275563892e-06, -1.6689286894688848e-06, -2.4318398573086597e-05, -0.00062851223628968, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -1.5616295058862306e-05, -8.344646857949556e-07, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -0.2014167457818985, -0.01604044809937477, -0.027265476062893867, -1.1324817933200393e-05, 0.0, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -7.033323527139146e-06, -8.344646857949556e-07, -4.827859811484814e-05, -3.6954811548639555e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -8.344646857949556e-07, -3.814689989667386e-06, -0.0003358753747306764, -0.00976086687296629, -0.0040906332433223724, -2.3841855067985307e-07, 0.0, -7.152555099310121e-07, -5.722029527532868e-06, -1.3232143828645349e-05, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -6.556489552167477e-06, -2.9802276912960224e-06, -5.602820692729438e-06, -6.437280717364047e-06, -9.536738616588991e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-